CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ each from its own NSDMI: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [class.base.init]/9.3: the base subobject L is default-initialized,
by L's default constructor (non-trivial, [class.default.ctor]/3), which
initializes L::next from its default member initializer ([class.base.init]/
9.1) -- also when D declares a member of the same name, which hides it
([class.member.lookup]).  The front
end initialized the base's members by looking up their names in D, so D's
initializer (7) went to D::next twice and L::next stayed nondeterministic.
g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG
assertion.
